# RENDERED .html EXPORTS of the .md files above (HXC-331, 2026-08-13).
#
# Root cause, not a symptom: this allowlist had ZERO .html globs, while the
# §11.4.65 doc-export wave touched 411 .html + 393 .pdf siblings in
# 437a893f (2026-06-10) — of which 408 .html / 390 .pdf were NEWLY ADDED and
# 3 of each were updates (`git diff-tree -r --diff-filter=A --name-only`
# vs the unfiltered form; both counts are right, they answer different
# questions, so state which one is meant). The .md SOURCES were allowlisted 2026-07-11 — two of
# the four explicitly (lines above), two via the `docs/superpowers/plans/*.md`
# wildcard — but their exports never were, so the SAME six key-shaped strings
# were suppressed in the source and flagged in the render. Verified 1:1: with
# the allowlist active the four .md sources scan rc=0; with no allowlist
# loadable they produce the same six findings. So this is NOT an export-only
# artefact and the remedy is NOT "fix the generator".
#
# EXPLICIT paths, never a `plans/*.html`-style wildcard — measured on this
# tree: a wildcard covering the directory ALSO silences a freshly planted
# key-shaped string in a sibling file (rc=0, silent), while these explicit
# entries leave that plant caught (rc=1). A wildcard here would trade six
# known-benign findings for an unbounded future blind spot.
#
# Honest boundary (§11.4.6): each entry blinds a scanner to the WHOLE file for
# all future content, which is why the set is four named files and not a glob.
# The six suppressed findings were each classified individually
# (fabricated/illustrative fixtures, no real credential).
#
# WHICH scanner these four entries actually bind, measured (an earlier revision
# of this comment asserted "BOTH scanners ... skip the .pdf siblings as binary";
# that is right about the outcome and wrong about the mechanism, so it is
# restated here rather than repeated):
#   * secret_scan.sh reads EVERY tracked file and decides per file. It is the
#     scanner these four entries operatively bind. It skips the .pdf siblings by
#     an explicit binary probe — `grep -Iq . -- <pdf>` -> rc=1 on all four — so
#     it never reads them. That one IS a binary skip.
#   * scan-secrets.sh never reaches EITHER extension in its real full-repo mode:
#     it restricts the walk with a `--include=` extension list (scan-secrets.sh
#     FILE_INCLUDES) that contains neither `.html` nor `.pdf`. That is an
#     extension filter, NOT a binary skip — the script has no binary probe at
#     all. Measured in that mode against fabricated plants: a `.md` plant is
#     flagged (rc=1, positive control) while `.html` and `.pdf`-named plants are
#     not. NOTE when re-testing: FILE_INCLUDES is applied ONLY when the scan
#     target is `.`; passing an explicit directory takes the else-branch, which
#     deliberately disables the filter for the test harness and WILL appear to
#     scan .html/.pdf. Testing that branch and concluding "it scans .html" is a
#     measurement artefact — it was made once already while writing this note.
#
# So "no finding on the .pdf siblings" is a statement about scanner REACH, not
# about the PDFs being clean. They are not clean: `pdftotext <pdf> | grep -cE -e
# '<key shapes>'` recovers 5 of the 6 suppressed strings across the four exports
# (the 6th only under a dash-tolerant variant — extraction mangles that file's
# dash runs; a 31,919-byte extraction confirms the reader itself worked). Use
# `grep -e` when probing: these patterns begin with `-----` and grep parses a
# bare pattern as a flag, which returns an ERROR that reads exactly like a 0.
# Anything that makes those streams greppable — an uncompressed re-export, a
# content-extracting scanner, a change to FILE_INCLUDES — surfaces them.
docs/improvements/05_phase_0_evidence.html
docs/troubleshooting/guide.html
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-05-04-phase-0-foundation-cleanup.html
