# CLI-agent config installer redaction tests + their reproduction record
# (§11.4.135/§11.4.138 pre-commit guard), 2026-09-07.
#
# WHY THESE ARE FALSE POSITIVES: test_agent_configs.sh builds agent config
# files containing fabricated credentials for the SOLE PURPOSE of asserting
# that install_agent_configs.sh masks them. The literals are the test's INPUT
# and its expected-redaction list — the same string appears twice, once as the
# planted fixture and once in the `for leaked in ...` assertion that proves it
# was scrubbed. Flagged shapes: sk- (OpenAI), AIzaSy (Google), and a generic
# PRIVATE KEY header. None would authenticate against any service.
#
# WHY THE ALLOWLIST RATHER THAN AN INLINE MARKER: the scanner accepts either.
# An inline REDACTED/EXAMPLE marker would require editing the credential
# literals themselves — but several sit inside heredoc'd JSON where a shell
# comment is not possible, and mangling the bodies would make the fixtures
# LESS faithful to the real key shapes the redaction regex must match. That is
# the "mangle content just to satisfy this scanner" outcome this file's own
# design notes argue against.
#
# HONEST BOUNDARY (§11.4.6), same as the four .html entries above: each entry
# blinds the scanner to the WHOLE file for all future content. Both are named
# EXPLICITLY, never a `scripts/tests/*` or `docs/qa/**` glob, which would trade
# these known-benign findings for an unbounded future blind spot.
